## Magnesium — Evidence Summary

The NHS states that magnesium is a mineral that helps turn food into energy and ensures the parathyroid glands, which produce hormones important for bone health, work normally, according to [NHS Vitamins and minerals – Others](https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/vitamins-and-minerals/others). A Leeds Teaching Hospitals NHS Trust patient leaflet additionally states that magnesium is needed for muscle development and nerve function, and that it is a metallic element mainly found in the bones and is essential to life, per the [NHS magnesium test page](https://www.nhs.uk/tests-and-treatments/magnesium-test).

The NHS identifies food sources including spinach, nuts, and wholemeal bread. Hull University Teaching Hospitals NHS Trust and Leeds Teaching Hospitals NHS Trust patient leaflets expand this to include seeds, wholegrains, pulses, fish, meat, and dairy products. NHS Lanarkshire similarly lists green vegetables, nuts and seeds, wholegrains, and beans and lentils.

The NHS states that the reference intake for magnesium is 300 mg per day for men aged 19 to 64, and 270 mg per day for women aged 19 to 64. Regarding supplements, the NHS states that 400 mg or less per day from supplements is unlikely to cause harm, and that taking more than 400 mg for a short time can cause diarrhoea.

On the question of specific forms, NICE states in its evidence summary ESUOM4 that oral magnesium glycerophosphate does not hold marketing authorisation in the UK for any indication, making it an unlicensed medicine, and that no other oral magnesium supplements hold marketing authorisation in the UK for preventing recurrent hypomagnesaemia. NICE further notes, citing the BNF, that magnesium glycerophosphate tablets or liquid are listed as suitable unlicensed preparations, available from special-order manufacturers or specialist importing companies, and that it is the prescriber's responsibility to determine clinical need and suitability.

