**Vitamin D — Evidence Summary**

The NHS states that vitamin D helps regulate the amount of calcium and phosphate in the body, and that these nutrients are needed to keep bones, teeth and muscles healthy (NHS: https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/vitamins-and-minerals/vitamin-d). East Sussex Healthcare NHS Trust additionally notes that vitamin D has a role in immune function and cell division, and that it has effects on many different organs and tissues all over the body (https://www.esht.nhs.uk/leaflet/vitamin-d-in-orthopaedics).

Food sources identified by the NHS include oily fish (such as salmon, sardines and mackerel), egg yolks, and fortified foods such as some fat spreads and breakfast cereals (NHS: https://www.nhs.uk/live-well/bone-health/food-for-strong-bones).

Regarding reference intake, the NHS states that the Department of Health and Social Care recommends adults and children over 4 take a daily supplement containing 10 micrograms (400 IU) of vitamin D (https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/vitamins-and-minerals/vitamin-d).

On the question of upper limits, the NHS states: "Do not take more than 100 micrograms (4,000 IU) of vitamin D a day as it could be harmful" — this figure applies to adults, including pregnant and breastfeeding women and the elderly, and children aged 11 to 17 years (NHS: https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/vitamins-and-minerals/vitamin-d). The Buckinghamshire NHS Formulary records that both EFSA and the UK Scientific Advisory Committee on Nutrition (SACN) concluded that an upper limit of 4,000 IU (100 micrograms) per day is safe for adults and children over 11 years of age including pregnant and lactating women (https://www.bucksformulary.nhs.uk/docs/Guideline_785FM.pdf). A NICE expert review paper (Cancer Research UK expert paper for NICE) records that in Europe and the USA, safe upper limits have been set at 50 µg/day (2,000 IU) for adults, and notes these levels are described as controversial (https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/ph32/documents/expert-paper-3-vitamin-d2).

