**Vitamin D — Evidence Summary**

The NHS states that vitamin D helps regulate the amount of calcium and phosphate in the body, nutrients described as needed to keep bones, teeth, and muscles healthy (NHS: https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/vitamins-and-minerals/vitamin-d). A South West London NHS ICB document similarly describes vitamin D as important for bone health and needed to absorb calcium, phosphate, and other nutrients from the diet (South West London NHS ICB: https://swlimo.southwestlondon.icb.nhs.uk/wp-content/uploads/SWL-Vitamin-D-Guidelines-for-Adults-and-Children.pdf).

Food sources identified by the NHS include oily fish (salmon, sardines, mackerel), egg yolks, and fortified foods such as some fat spreads and breakfast cereals (NHS: https://www.nhs.uk/live-well/bone-health/food-for-strong-bones).

NICE guidance records reference nutrient intakes (RNI) for specific life stages — 8.5 µg/day for infants up to six months, 7 µg/day for children between six months and three years, and 10 µg/day for adults aged 65 and over and during pregnancy and lactation — and notes that currently there is no RNI for people aged between 4 and 65 (NICE: https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/ph56/resources/implementing-vitamin-d-guidance-draft-guideline2). NHS sources reference a standard supplement figure of 10 micrograms (400 IU) per day for the general adult population (NHS Cornwall and Isles of Scilly ICB: https://cios.icb.nhs.uk/health/nutrition/vitamin-d).

Regarding upper limits, the Buckinghamshire NHS Formulary document states, citing EFSA and the UK Scientific Advisory Committee on Nutrition (SACN), that an upper limit of 4,000 IU (100 micrograms) per day is considered safe for adults and children over 11 years, including pregnant and lactating women (Buckinghamshire NHS Formulary: https://www.bucksformulary.nhs.uk/docs/Guideline_785FM.pdf). The Barnsley NHS document similarly records the EFSA upper limit as 100 micrograms (4,000 IU) per day (Barnsley NHS CCG: https://best.barnsleyccg.nhs.uk/media/5njn2wrf/vit_d_management_guideline.pdf). A Royal Osteoporosis Society document published via RUH Bath NHS Foundation Trust states that high intakes for prolonged periods can be toxic and notes the figure of 100 micrograms per day in that context (RUH Bath NHS Foundation Trust: https://ruh.nhs.uk/patients/services/hip_fracture_management/documents/ROS_Vitamin_D_Supplements.pdf).

