**Vitamin D – Evidence Summary**

The NHS states that vitamin D "helps regulate the amount of calcium and phosphate in the body" and that these nutrients "are needed to keep bones, teeth and muscles healthy" (https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/vitamins-and-minerals/vitamin-d). Barnsley CCG (NHS) records that vitamin D "promotes calcium absorption from the bowel, enables mineralisation in bone and plays an important role in muscle function" (https://best.barnsleyccg.nhs.uk/clinical-support/medicines/prescribing-guidelines/Vit_D_Management_Guideline.pdf). East Sussex Healthcare NHS Trust notes that vitamin D "also has a role in immune function and cell division" and "has effects on many different organs and tissues all over the body" (https://www.esht.nhs.uk/leaflet/vitamin-d-in-orthopaedics).

The NHS lists oily fish (such as salmon, sardines, and mackerel), egg yolks, and fortified foods such as some fat spreads and breakfast cereals as food sources of vitamin D (https://www.nhs.uk/live-well/bone-health/food-for-strong-bones). NHS Cornwall and Isles of Scilly additionally notes that most vitamin D comes from the action of sunlight on the skin (https://cios.icb.nhs.uk/health/nutrition/vitamin-d).

The NHS and NICE both record a reference nutrient intake of 10 micrograms (400 IU) per day for adults, with NICE noting there is currently no reference nutrient intake set for people aged between 4 and 65 outside specific population groups (https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/ph56/resources/implementing-vitamin-d-guidance-draft-guideline2).

Regarding upper limits, the NHS states: "Do not take more than 100 micrograms (4,000 IU) of vitamin D a day as it could be harmful. This applies to adults, including pregnant and breastfeeding women and the elderly, and children aged 11 to 17 years." The NHS also states that "taking too many vitamin D supplements over a long period of time can cause too much calcium to build up in the body (hypercalcaemia)" (https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/vitamins-and-minerals/vitamin-d). The Buckinghamshire Formulary (NHS) records that the UK Scientific Advisory Committee on Nutrition (SACN) "concluded that an upper limit of 4,000 International Units (100 micrograms) per day is safe for adults and children over 11 years of age including pregnant and lactating women" (https://www.bucksformulary.nhs.uk/docs/Guideline_785FM.pdf).

