## Vitamin D — Evidence Summary

The NHS states that vitamin D helps regulate the amount of calcium and phosphate in the body, and that these nutrients are needed to keep bones, teeth and muscles healthy (https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/vitamins-and-minerals/vitamin-d). East Sussex Healthcare NHS Trust adds that vitamin D also has a role in immune function and cell division (https://www.esht.nhs.uk/leaflet/vitamin-d-in-orthopaedics).

The NHS lists oily fish (such as salmon, sardines and mackerel), egg yolks, and fortified foods such as some fat spreads and breakfast cereals as good food sources (https://www.nhs.uk/live-well/bone-health/food-for-strong-bones). NHS Cornwall and Isles of Scilly further notes that meat and milk contain a small amount, and that margarine and some breakfast cereals have vitamin D added (https://cios.icb.nhs.uk/health/nutrition/vitamin-d).

On reference intake, NICE records that there is currently no Reference Nutrient Intake for people aged between 4 and 65, while a figure of 10 µg/day applies to older adults (65 and over) and during pregnancy and lactation (https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/ph56/resources/implementing-vitamin-d-guidance-draft-guideline2). NHS Cornwall and Isles of Scilly notes that Public Health England recommends everyone over the age of one considers taking a supplement of 10 micrograms (400 IU) per day during autumn and winter months (https://cios.icb.nhs.uk/health/nutrition/vitamin-d).

Regarding upper limits, the NHS states that adults, including pregnant and breastfeeding women and the elderly, and children aged 11 to 17, should not take more than 100 micrograms (4,000 IU) of vitamin D a day, as it could be harmful (https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/vitamins-and-minerals/vitamin-d). The Buckinghamshire ICB document records that both the European Food Safety Authority (EFSA) and the UK Scientific Advisory Committee on Nutrition (SACN) concluded that an upper limit of 4,000 IU (100 micrograms) per day is safe for adults and children over 11 years of age, including pregnant and lactating women (https://www.bucksformulary.nhs.uk/docs/Guideline_785FM.pdf).

