## Vitamin B12 — Sourced Evidence Summary

According to the NHS, Vitamin B12 helps the body make red blood cells, keeps the nervous system healthy, releases energy from food, and helps the body use folate. The NHS similarly states, in its page on cyanocobalamin, that the body needs vitamin B12 to make red blood cells and keep the nervous system healthy.

The NHS lists good food sources of Vitamin B12 as meat, fish, milk, cheese, eggs, and some fortified breakfast cereals, and notes that vitamin B12 is not found naturally in foods such as fruit, vegetables, and grains. NHS Lanarkshire additionally lists milk and dairy products, eggs, oily fish, liver, yellow/red/green vegetables, and yellow fruit (such as mango) as sources.

The NHS states that adults aged 19 to 64 need about 1.5 micrograms of vitamin B12 per day. This figure is corroborated by NHS 111 Wales.

Regarding supplemental intake, the NHS states that taking 2 mg or less a day of vitamin B12 in supplements is unlikely to cause any harm, and that there is not enough evidence to show what the effects may be of taking high doses each day.

On regulatory status, the NHS states that hydroxocobalamin is only available on prescription and is given as an injection. A UKMI Medicines Q&A document published via the NHS Specialist Pharmacy Service notes that cyanocobalamin oral preparations are listed on the Blacklist of UK drug tariffs, and that some preparations are available to purchase as food supplements while others are licensed medicines.

