## Vitamin B12 — Evidence Summary

The NHS states that the body needs vitamin B12 "to make red blood cells and keep your nervous system healthy," and that vitamin B12 and folate "perform several important functions in the body, including keeping the nervous system healthy" (NHS, *About cyanocobalamin*: https://www.nhs.uk/medicines/cyanocobalamin/about-cyanocobalamin; NHS, *Vitamin B12 or folate deficiency anaemia*: https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/vitamin-b12-or-folate-deficiency-anaemia).

According to the NHS, good dietary sources include meat, fish, milk, cheese, eggs, and some fortified breakfast cereals; the NHS also notes that vitamin B12 is not found naturally in fruits, vegetables, or grains (NHS, *B vitamins and folic acid*: https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/vitamins-and-minerals/vitamin-b). An NHS Lanarkshire patient leaflet additionally lists milk and dairy products, eggs, oily fish, and liver among its sources (https://www.nhslanarkshire.scot.nhs.uk/patient-information-leaflets/nutrition-dietetics/pil-fodsrc-21-12986-l).

The NHS states that adults aged 19 to 64 need about 1.5 micrograms of vitamin B12 per day (NHS, *B vitamins and folic acid*: https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/vitamins-and-minerals/vitamin-b), a figure corroborated by NHS 111 Wales (http://111.wales.nhs.uk/encyclopaedia/v/article/Vitaminsandminerals) and an NHS NW ICB patient information leaflet (https://nwknowledgenow.nhs.uk/wp-content/uploads/2025/07/Oral-treatment-of-Vitamin-B12-deficiency_PIL.pdf).

On upper limits, the NHS states there is not enough evidence to show the effects of taking high doses of vitamin B12 supplements each day, and that taking 2 mg or less per day in supplements is unlikely to cause harm (NHS, *B vitamins and folic acid*: https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/vitamins-and-minerals/vitamin-b).

Regarding regulatory status, the NHS describes cyanocobalamin as "a manufactured version of vitamin B12" available as a medicine (https://www.nhs.uk/medicines/cyanocobalamin/about-cyanocobalamin). The Greater Manchester Formulary notes that lower-strength cyanocobalamin tablets (100 and 500 micrograms) are available over the counter and are marked "do not prescribe" on the NHS, as they are considered suitable only for prevention or maintenance of dietary deficiency (https://www.greatermanchesterformulary.nhs.uk/chaptersSubDetails.asp?FormularySectionID=9&SubSectionRef=09.06.02&SubSectionID=A100). The Bedfordshire and Luton Joint Formulary states that hydroxocobalamin has completely replaced cyanocobalamin as the form of choice for therapy, as it is retained in the body longer (https://www.bedsformulary.nhs.uk/chaptersSubDetails.asp?FormularySectionID=9&SubSectionRef=09.01&SubSectionID=A100).

